Marik Sahamin (born 5 June 1977) is a Burawi politician and electrical engineer who served as the [[Leader of the Burawi Republic]] from 2015 to 2025. He is the founder and current leader of the [[Forward Party of Burawa]], and previously represented [[Badalga-6]] in the [[Lower House of Burawa]] from 2007 until his election to the Leadership. At age 38, he became the youngest Leader in Burawi history following his victory in the [[2015 Burawi leadership election]].

Sahamin's decade in office was marked by ambitious policy initiatives, including the recognition of [[same-sex marriage]] in 2016, an 8 per cent public sector wage increase in 2016 — the largest in Burawi history — the [[Smart Transportation Act of 2016]] mandating electric public transit vehicles, the [[Clean Air and Transportation Act of 2020]] which banned domestic commercial flights, the [[2018 Drug Reform Act]] legalising medicinal marijuana and mushrooms, the [[2018 Alternative Energy Mandate]] creating a 100 billion [[Hanti]] clean energy fund, the creation of the [[Digital Hanti]] cryptocurrency, the [[2022 Artificial Alignment Bill]] creating a 50 billion hanti AI investment fund, the resolution of the Kwalib-Kushish-Burawa border dispute, and the [[2022 Sabia operation]]. He became the first Burawi Leader to visit 100 countries in 2018. His government managed Burawa's response to the [[COVID-19 pandemic]], instituted collective sanctions following the [[Burawi High Speed Rail Scandal|HSR Scandal]], reduced mandatory military service from two years to 1.5 years, restricted high-end GPU exports under the [[2023 Export Control Act]], and reformed the immigration system. His foreign policy emphasised the concept of a "Burawi sphere of influence" — active economic and political management of neighbouring Kaftian states. Burawa experienced a period of elevated inflation from 2021 to 2023, contributing to public dissatisfaction in his later years.

In February 2026, Sahamin was removed as party leader by a vote of no confidence. He announced his candidacy in the [[2026 Burawi snap leadership election|snap leadership election]] in March 2026, triggering a party split that resulted in the formation of the [[Forward Progressive Party]]. He is a candidate in the 2026 snap election.

== Early life and education ==

Marik Sahamin was born on 5 June 1977 in [[Badalga]], a major city in the [[Lake Haro]] region of Burawa. His mother, [[Amina Sahamin]], was a professor at the [[University of Badalga]]; his father was a civil servant. He was raised in the Badalga area and attended Badalga Central High School, where he served as president of the student union.

After completing secondary education, Sahamin entered the [[Burawi Work Programme]] through the continued education track and enrolled at the [[People's University of Burawa]] in [[Kankadadka]], where he studied electrical engineering.

== Early career ==

Following graduation, Sahamin was hired by [[Qynos]], a multinational computer electronics company based in [[Khar]]. He worked in the technology sector during the early 2000s.

Sahamin became politically active in his early twenties, joining his local community council in Badalga. He subsequently served as a political aide to [[Siciid Barre]], the popular senator for Badalga, gaining experience in electoral politics and constituent services.

== Parliamentary career ==

=== Election to the Lower House ===

At age 28, Sahamin contested the [[Badalga-6]] legislative district in the [[2007 Burawi Lower House election]], winning his first term in the [[Lower House of Burawa]]. The district encompassed the neighbourhood where he had grown up. He was elected as a member of the [[Socialist Party of Burawa]] and aligned himself with the party's progressive wing.

=== Rise within the Socialist Party ===

Over the following years, Sahamin built support within the progressive faction of the Socialist Party. He advocated for faster reform on social issues, environmental policy, and government modernisation. By the early 2010s, tensions had emerged between the progressive wing and the party's establishment, which Sahamin and his allies criticised as overly cautious.

In 2014, Sahamin broke from the Socialist Party to found the [[Forward Party of Burawa]] on 4 July. The new party positioned itself as a vehicle for ambitious progressive action and attracted support from reformers, environmentalists, and younger voters.

== Leader of the Burawi Republic (2015–2025) ==

{{main|Leadership of Marik Sahamin}}

=== 2015 election ===

{{main|2015 Burawi leadership election}}

Sahamin contested the [[2015 Burawi leadership election]] as the Forward Party candidate. The election was a triple election in which the Leadership, the [[Senate of Burawa|Senate]], and the [[Lower House of Burawa|Lower House]] were all contested simultaneously — a convergence that occurs every thirty years. At age 38, Sahamin won the election, becoming the youngest [[Leader of the Burawi Republic]] in Burawi history.

=== Economic and technology policy ===

Upon taking office in 2015, Sahamin shifted Burawa's industrial investment focus from traditional hardware manufacturing to software development. His government established innovation councils to coordinate technology policy and introduced government-funded startup incubators to support the growth of a domestic software industry. The initiative represented a departure from Burawa's historical emphasis on heavy industry and manufacturing.

In 2017, Sahamin's government launched the Digital Hanti, a domestic cryptocurrency pegged to the Burawi [[hanti]]. The digital currency was intended to modernise the financial system and facilitate electronic transactions. However, in 2021, the government enacted harsh restrictions on cryptocurrency speculation, banning the sale of [[NFT]]s and other digital assets pegged to non-state cryptocurrencies. The restrictions were framed as consumer protection measures but drew criticism from technology entrepreneurs and digital rights advocates.

=== Artificial intelligence policy ===

In 2022, Sahamin signed the [[2022 Artificial Alignment Bill]], creating a 50 billion [[hanti]] fund to invest in artificial intelligence research and development. The following year, he established the Burawi Artificial Intelligence Research Lab at the [[University of Kaysoor]], with a mandate to develop domestic [[large language model]]s and reduce Burawa's dependence on foreign AIAlso in 2023, Sahamin signed the [[2023 Export Control Act]], which restricted the export of certain high-end [[GPU|graphics processing unit]] models from Burawa. The legislation aimed to prevent advanced computing technology from being used by adversary states and to protect Burawa's competitive advantage in AI development.

In 2024, Sahamin's government issued the [[2024 AI Import Restriction Mandate]], which restricted government agencies from using non-domestic large language models following the launch of BuChat 1.2, a Burawi-developed AI assistant. The mandate was part of a broader technology sovereignty strategy to insulate Burawa's digital infrastructure from foreign influencedevelopment.

=== Public sector wages ===

In 2016, Sahamin's government enacted a public sector wage increase of 8 per cent — the largest in Burawi history at the time. The increase was criticised as economically reckless by fiscal conservatives, particularly given the already-ambitious spending programmes in other areas. For the remainder of the decade, wage increases were moderated to approximately 2 per cent annually.

Following the onset of the COVID-19 pandemic, Sahamin's government authorised a 5 per cent wage increase in 2020 and 4 per cent in 2021. These increases, combined with pandemic-related supply chain disruptions, contributed to a period of elevated inflation from 2021 to 2023.

=== Energy policy ===

The [[2018 Alternative Energy Mandate]] established a 100 billion [[hanti]] fund to transition Burawa's electricity generation away from [[petroleum|oil]] and [[natural gas]] toward renewable sources. The mandate represented one of the largest single investments in clean energy infrastructure in Burawi history and sought to reduce the country's dependence on fossil fuel exports for domestic power generation.

=== Drug policy ===

The [[2018 Drug Reform Act]] liberalised Burawa's drug laws, allowing the medicinal sale of [[psilocybin mushroom]]s and legalising the consumption of [[marijuana]]. The legislation created the [[Burawi Medicinal Substance Distribution Company]], a state-owned enterprise responsible for managing the sale of marijuana for personal use and mushrooms for prescription medical purposes. The reform was part of Sahamin's broader agenda of social liberalisation and public health modernisation.

=== Military service ===

In 2019, Sahamin's government reduced the mandatory [[Burawi Work Programme|military service obligation]] from two years to 1.5 years. The reduction was framed as an efficiency measure, reflecting improvements in training methodology and technological advancement in the armed forces. The policy was popular among younger voters but drew criticism from military traditionalists who argued it would reduce readiness.

=== Transportation policy ===

The [[Smart Transportation Act of 2016]] mandated that all new public transit vehicles purchased by the state must be electric, with a target of ending the purchase of non-electric vehicles by 2025. The legislation was part of Sahamin's broader environmental agenda to reduce carbon emissions and modernise Burawa's transportation infrastructure.

In 2020, Sahamin's government passed the [[Clean Air and Transportation Act of 2020]], which banned most domestic commercial flights between Burawi cities in favour of the [[Burawi High Speed Rail|high-speed rail]] network. The legislation drew criticism from rural communities, where residents were required to travel to metropolitan airports to access the rail network. The policy was later partially reversed by his successor, [[Hancil Thea]], through executive directive reopening military airfields for civilian use.

=== Foreign policy ===

Sahamin pursued an activist foreign policy characterised by the concept of a Burawi sphere of influence — the active economic and political management of neighbouring Kaftian states and former colonies.

In 2016, Sahamin became the first Burawi Leader to make an official state visit to [[Tamazgha]], signalling a strengthening of diplomatic and economic ties between the two nation Later that year, his government negotiated a five-year plan to resolve the contested [[Kwalib]]-[[Kushish]]-Burawa border dispute. A treaty was signed by all three parties establishing a new internationally recognised border. The agreement formally recognised Kwalib as an independent state and included a reparations package for residents of the formerly contested territory who had been affected by the decades-long dispute.

In 2018, Sahamin became the first Burawi Leader to visit 100 countries across Adonia, marking the most extensive diplomatic tour by any Burawi head of state. The achievement underscored Sahamin's emphasis on international engagement and Burawa's growing role in global affairste.

The most controversial expression of Sahamin's foreign policy was the [[2022 Sabia operation]], a one-day military intervention to depose the president of [[Sabia]]. The operation generated significant domestic debate but succeeded in installing a government aligned with Burawi interests.

=== Military affairs ===

In 2017, following the [[2017 Khar Army Base shooting]], in which a mentally unstable soldier murdered five fellow troops, Sahamin ordered the closure of the [[Khar Army Base]]. The base, one of the oldest in the Burawi military, was deemed obsolete. The facility was converted into residential housing and a memorial to the victims of the shooting.

=== HSR Scandal ===

{{main|Burawi High Speed Rail Scandal}}

In 2019, the [[Burawi High Speed Rail Scandal]] was exposed, revealing that northern rural representatives had conspired to secure billions of [[hanti]] in funding for a high-speed rail line between [[Sago]] and [[Lanwaley]] based on falsified population projections. The completed route recorded the lowest ridership of any HSR line in the country. Sahamin's government responded with collective sanctions against the entire [[Dhulsarren]] region, including funding restrictions and heightened scrutiny of regional representatives. The sanctions were perceived in the Dhulsarren as disproportionate collective punishment and contributed to the political rise of [[Hancil Thea]], who emerged as the region's principal advocate during the crisis.

=== COVID-19 pandemic ===

{{main|COVID-19 pandemic in Burawa}}

The [[COVID-19 pandemic]] reached Burawa in early 2020. Sahamin's government instituted an immediate nationwide lockdown, which was initially popular and credited with limiting the spread of the virus. However, as the pandemic continued into 2021 and 2022, public fatigue with restrictions grew. Protests against the lockdown measures increased in frequency, and Sahamin's approval ratings declined.

Restrictions were fully lifted in 2022, but the prolonged emergency measures had contributed to growing dissatisfaction with Sahamin's government. The pandemic response, combined with the HSR Scandal sanctions, the Sabia operation, and the Clean Air act, formed the backdrop for his electoral defeat in 2025.

=== Immigration reform ===

In 2019, Sahamin's government reformed the [[Immigration in Burawa|immigration system]], introducing a 90-day grace period for foreign workers who lost employment, replacing the previous requirement to leave the country and restart the ten-year citizenship path entirely.

=== Same-sex marriage ===

One of the Forward Party's first legislative acts was the passage of a resolution recognising [[same-sex marriage]] in 2016. The Socialist Party, while supportive in principle, had declined to legislate marriage equality during its decades in power; Sahamin's government acted within its first year.

=== Party consolidation ===

In 2020, the [[Progressive Socialist Party of Burawa]] — formerly the principal vehicle of the progressive coalition — formally merged with the Forward Party, consolidating much of the progressive electorate under Sahamin's leadership.

=== 2025 election defeat ===

{{main|2025 Burawi leadership election}}

Sahamin sought re-election in the [[2025 Burawi leadership election]], the first leadership election conducted under [[ranked-choice voting]]. He placed third in first-preference votes with 24 per cent, behind [[Xasan Garaad]] (30 per cent) and [[Hancil Thea]] (28 per cent). Under the RCV redistribution, Thea accumulated sufficient second-preference support to win the election. The result ended a decade of Forward Party government and marked the first time the [[National Party of Burawa]] had won the Leadership.

== Post-Leadership (2025–2026) ==

Following his electoral defeat, Sahamin remained the leader of the Forward Party and retained his seat representing Badalga-6. The Forward Party continued to hold a majority in the [[Senate of Burawa|Senate]] (128 seats), a legacy of its strength in the 2024 Senate election, though this was expected to diminish in the 2027 election.

=== Vote of no confidence ===

In February 2026, the Forward Party congress held a vote of no confidence in Sahamin's leadership, which passed. [[Warsame Cilmi]] was elected as his successor, pledging to refocus the party on its reformist identity. Sahamin remained a prominent figure within the party but no longer held the formal leadership.

In the weeks following his removal, Sahamin worked to regain political influence within the Forward Party. He negotiated an agreement to run as the party's candidate for Leader in a future election, but this arrangement was opposed by the faction that had supported his removal.

=== Snap election candidacy ===

{{main|2026 Burawi snap election}}

On 26 March 2026, following the successful recall of [[Hancil Thea]] from the Leadership, Sahamin announced his candidacy in the [[2026 Burawi snap leadership election|snap leadership election]]. The announcement triggered a major split within the Forward Party. Approximately 60 per cent of the party's parliamentary caucus — comprising primarily those who had supported the February no-confidence vote — defected to form the [[Forward Progressive Party]] under [[Mahad Allaale]]. 

Sahamin retained the support of approximately 40 per cent of the caucus and reclaimed the party leadership. The Forward Progressive Party announced it would not field its own candidate and instead endorsed [[Muna Xasan]] of the [[Party for Equitable Progress]]. The split left Sahamin's Forward Party with 51 Senate seats and 40 Lower House seats.

Sahamin has participated in televised debates on 8 April and 12 April 2026. Opinion polls have recorded his support at 10 per cent (7 April), 4 per cent (9 April), and 8 per cent (12 April), placing him fourth among the candidates.

== Political positions ==

Sahamin is associated with the progressive wing of Burawi politics. His political positions reflect the platform of the [[Forward Party of Burawa]], which he founded in 2014 as a vehicle for reformist and socially liberal policies.

On social issues, Sahamin advocates for [[LGBTQ rights in Burawa|LGBTQ+ rights]], feminist policy, and expanded social services. His government's early recognition of same-sex marriage in 2016 signalled a departure from the Socialist Party's more cautious approach to social liberalisation. The 2018 Drug Reform Act, which legalised marijuana and medicinal mushrooms, further demonstrated his commitment to progressive social policy.

On environmental policy, Sahamin championed the transition from domestic aviation to high-speed rail through the Clean Air and Transportation Act of 2020, and the electrification of public transit through the Smart Transportation Act of 2016. The 2018 Alternative Energy Mandate reflected his commitment to reducing Burawa's dependence on fossil fuels.

On foreign policy, Sahamin supports an active Burawi role in neighbouring states, articulated through the concept of a "Burawi sphere of influence." The 2022 Sabia operation represented the most assertive expression of this doctrine, intervening militarily to depose a government deemed hostile to Burawi interests.

On immigration, Sahamin advocates for expanded worker protections within the immigration system, as demonstrated by the 2019 reform introducing a 90-day grace period for workers who lost employment.

On economic policy, Sahamin supports selective liberalisation in technology sectors — including software development, artificial intelligence, and digital currencies — while maintaining essential industries under state control. His government's restrictions on non-state cryptocurrencies in 2021 reflected a balance between technological modernisation and state oversight of financial systems.

== Personal life ==

Sahamin married [[Xalwo Geedi]], his college sweetheart, in 2000. The couple have two children and reside in [[Badalga]].

